From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] the NTSC rate from the exact master clock, not a rounded one

The NTSC frame rate came from a master clock rounded to 21477272 Hz. The
SNES's is 945/44 MHz = 21477272.727..., six times the NTSC subcarrier, and
the rounding leaves the reported rate 0.034 ppm low.

Inaudible on its own. It matters here because the panel mode this core
selects is cut to twice the rate the core reports, and the mode is a whole
number of kHz of pixel clock: 174651000 / (1302 x 1116) = 120.197628 Hz.
Against the rounded clock that mode is +0.037 ppm, a frame of drift every
63 hours; against the exact one it is +0.003 ppm, a frame every 824 hours.
Nestopia computes the NES's identical clock exactly already, which is why
one mode serves both - and why, before this, the mode was exact for the
NES and a near miss for the SNES it is named after.

PAL is unchanged: 21281370 Hz is already the exact PAL master clock.

--- a/libretro/libretro.cpp
+++ b/libretro/libretro.cpp
@@ -1150,7 +1150,12 @@
     info->timing.sample_rate = msu1_enhanced_active()
                              ? (double) msu1_enhanced_output_rate()
                              : (double) S9xGetAudioSampleRate();
-    info->timing.fps = retro_get_region() == RETRO_REGION_NTSC ? 21477272.0 / 357366.0 : 21281370.0 / 425568.0;
+    /* NTSC: 945/44 MHz, six times the subcarrier, over a frame of
+       1364 x 262 - 2 master clocks. The 21477272 Hz this rounded to
+       sits 0.034 ppm low, which is enough to miss a panel mode cut
+       to twice the reported rate. PAL's 21281370 Hz is already the
+       exact master clock, 4.8 x the 4.43361875 MHz subcarrier. */
+    info->timing.fps = retro_get_region() == RETRO_REGION_NTSC ? (945000000.0 / 44.0) / 357366.0 : 21281370.0 / 425568.0;
 
     g_screen_gun_width = width;
     g_screen_gun_height = height;
